              "passageTitle": "Cleaning the Ocean",
              "text": "Somewhere in the Pacific Ocean between California and Hawaii, there is an area where ocean currents collect huge amounts of floating plastic. It is often called the Great Pacific Garbage Patch. Scientists estimate that it covers an area about three times the size of France, although it is not a solid island of rubbish. Most of the plastic is made up of small pieces floating near the surface.\n\nIn 2013, a Dutch teenager named Boyan Slat decided to do something about it. While diving on holiday in Greece, he had seen more plastic bags than fish. He wondered whether it was possible to use the ocean's own currents to collect the rubbish. At just eighteen, he founded an organisation called The Ocean Cleanup and asked the public for money. Thousands of people supported his idea online.\n\nThe first systems did not work as planned. A long floating barrier tested in 2018 broke after a few months and could not hold the plastic. Instead of giving up, the team studied the problem, made changes and tried again. Their newer systems are pulled slowly by boats and guide the plastic into a large net. When the net is full, it is lifted onto a ship and the plastic is taken to land to be recycled.\n\nSlat knows that cleaning the ocean is only half of the solution. Most plastic reaches the sea through rivers, so the organisation also places floating machines called Interceptors in rivers to catch rubbish before **it** reaches the ocean. Critics say the projects are expensive and can only remove a small part of the plastic. Supporters reply that every tonne removed matters, and that the project has made millions of people more aware of the problem.",
